    "title": "Uganda Announces Full Military Withdrawal from Somalia by August 2027",
    "original_title": "Uganda oo Shaacisay Xilliga ay Dhammaan Ciidamadeeda Kala Baxayso Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "uganda-announces-full-military-withdrawal-from-somalia-by-august-2027",
    "summary": "Uganda's army chief, General Muhoozi Kainerugaba, declared that all Ugandan troops will leave Somalia by August 1, 2027, ending nearly two decades of deployment. The announcement comes as Somalia's security transition advances under the new AU mission.",
    "content": "Uganda's military leadership has confirmed a complete withdrawal of its forces from Somalia, setting a firm deadline of August 1, 2027. Army chief General Muhoozi Kainerugaba announced the timeline on his social media account on August 28, stating that no Ugandan troops will remain in Somalia beyond that date. This marks the first time Uganda has publicly specified an end date for its military presence in the Horn of Africa nation.\n\nUganda has been a key contributor to peacekeeping efforts in Somalia since 2007, when it became the first country to deploy troops under the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM). Over the years, Ugandan forces have operated in various regions, supporting the Somali government against militant groups. The announcement signals a shift in Uganda's long-term military commitment, which has been a cornerstone of regional security cooperation.\n\nThe withdrawal is scheduled to occur as Somalia prepares to take over full security responsibilities from international forces. The African Union's current mission, known as ATMIS, is gradually handing over security tasks to Somali forces, with a broader drawdown planned by the end of 2025. Uganda's decision aligns with this broader transition, though the exact timeline may be adjusted based on conditions on the ground.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development raises questions about the future stability of their homeland. Many diaspora members have relied on Ugandan troops' presence as a stabilizing factor in regions like Mogadishu and Middle Shabelle. The withdrawal could create security gaps, potentially affecting diaspora investments and plans for return. At the same time, it underscores the progress Somalia has made in rebuilding its own military capabilities, and diaspora members may see this as a call to support national security forces through investment and advocacy.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/29/uganda-oo-shaacisay-xilliga-ay-dhammaan-ciidamadeeda-kala-baxayso-soomaaliya/",
