    "summary": "Over 200 families from rural Mudug have moved to displacement camps in Galkayo after losing their livestock, where they struggle with inadequate food, water, and shelter.",
    "content": "More than 200 families from rural areas of central Somalia's Mudug region have relocated to displacement camps in Galkayo after losing their livestock to drought and other hardships. The families, who arrived between April and August, are now facing severe shortages of basic necessities.\n\nAmong them is Fardowso Nur Mohamed, a mother of six, who has been living in Horiyo camp since April. She described arriving with virtually no belongings and no relatives to rely on. Her family often goes to bed hungry and depends on the goodwill of other camp residents, many of whom are equally strained.\n\nThe influx adds pressure to already resource-poor camps that lack adequate water, sanitation, and health services. Local authorities and aid agencies have noted the growing needs but have been unable to meet them due to funding gaps.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this situation underscores the fragility of pastoral livelihoods in the region and the urgent need for sustained humanitarian support. Many diaspora members send remittances to relatives in such camps, but the scale of displacement requires coordinated relief efforts.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/08/impoverished-pastoralists-in-mudug-flee-to-resource-poor-camps-in-galkayo/",
