    "content": "A magnitude 6.0 earthquake struck the Gulf of Aden at approximately 12:07 AM local time on August 28, 2026, centered about 145 kilometers north of Caluula, a coastal town in Puntland's Bari region. The United States Geological Survey (USGS) reported the epicenter was located beneath the seabed at an estimated depth of 10 kilometers, classifying the shaking intensity as level IV on the Modified Mercalli Intensity scale.\n\nReports indicate the tremors were felt in several coastal areas of Somalia, particularly in Puntland, as well as in neighboring Yemen across the Gulf. While initial assessments have not reported any significant damage or casualties, residents in the affected areas experienced noticeable shaking, causing some to evacuate their homes as a precaution.\n\nThis earthquake is part of the ongoing seismic activity associated with the divergence of the Arabian and African tectonic plates along the Gulf of Aden and the East African Rift system. The region has a history of moderate to strong earthquakes, though undersea events like this one typically pose less risk to coastal communities than those on land.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, seismic events in the Horn of Africa can raise concerns for families and property in Puntland. While this earthquake appears to have been relatively benign, it underscores the importance of emergency preparedness in a region prone to natural hazards. Authorities in Puntland have yet to issue an official statement, but monitoring agencies are continuing to assess the situation.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/28/dhul-gariir-m6-0-ah-oo-ka-dhacay-gacanka-cadmeed-meel-u-dhow-xeebaha-puntland/",
