    "summary": "Jubaland's Ministry of Education has released primary school leaving exam results, with a 99% pass rate among 5,778 candidates. The top ten positions were largely achieved by female students.",
    "content": "The Ministry of Education, Culture and Higher Education of Jubaland State has released the results of the Grade 8 certificate examination for the 2025/2026 academic year. According to the announcement, 5,778 students sat for the exam, and 5,720 passed, representing a success rate of 99 percent. This leaves 58 students, or 1 percent, who did not pass.\n\nThe results highlight a notable achievement for female candidates, as the top ten positions in the exam were predominantly secured by girls. This outcome is part of a broader trend in Jubaland and across Somalia, where girls have increasingly outperformed boys in national examinations in recent years.\n\nThe exam is the culmination of the primary education cycle, and passing it is essential for students to transition to secondary school. The high pass rate suggests that the majority of candidates are prepared for the next stage of their education.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this news is significant as it provides insights into the educational standards and progress within Jubaland. Many diaspora families send remittances to support relatives in education, and some may be planning to enroll children in schools back home. Understanding the performance of the education system helps diaspora members make informed decisions about educational investments in Somalia.\n\nThe Ministry has not yet specified the date for the next academic year's enrollment or the process for students who did not pass to retake the exam. However, the ministry encourages all students, both successful and unsuccessful, to continue their efforts in pursuit of their educational goals.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/27/jubaland-oo-shaacisay-natiijada-imtixaanka-shahaadiga-fasalka-8-aad/",
